  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periods of unsettled weather are expected\nthrough Friday.\n\nA longwave trough axis will move from the upper Midwest to the \nwestern Canadian Maritimes throughout the remainder of the work \nweek. Meanwhile, a few shortwave troughs will pass across the region \nsupporting continued west-southwesterly to northwesterly cyclonic \nflow. At the surface, a cold front will continue to advance \nsoutheast across the region this morning, not only will this help \npush the shower activity over north-central New York to exit east \nthis morning, the breezy winds will also continue to diminish.\n\nLow-level cold air advection in the wake of the cold front today \nwill support a few periodic lake-effect rain showers over and east-\nsoutheast of Lake America, mainly impacting Oswego county and the \nTug Hill Plateau due to the predominantly westerly flow. An isolated \nrain shower or two will be possible this afternoon into early \nevening across the Niagara Frontier, Genesee Valley and into north-\ncentral New York as weak potential instability within the lowest 1km \nof the atmosphere is released from low-level convergence and moist \nascent along the surface trough axes from the shortwave trough \naloft. Otherwise, primarily fair weather is expected for the \nremainder of the area through Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remnants of Hurricane Isaias will bring a soaking \nrain to the region Sunday.\n\nWhile still a few days out and the pattern evolution continues to \nremain uncertain, remnants of Hurricane Isaias looks to make its way \nnortheast across the Southeast Coast and towards the Mid-Atlantic \nthis weekend. The northernmost reaches of the low pressure system \nwill likely bring a widespread soaking rain to the region by Sunday \nand last through Sunday night. As alluded to previously, rainfall \namounts continue to remain uncertain at this time, so stay tuned for \nfuture forecasts.",
